# Match-three behaviour.
#
# Total and non-recursive: definitions are inlined by the compiler and 'for' is a
# counted loop, so there is no recursion and every program terminates. State
# lives in the scene's slot table; @name reads a slot and !name writes one, while
# bare '@' and '!' take the slot index from the stack and '&name' pushes it.
#
# The engine knows nothing about boards. It has an entity pool, and each entity
# is an animation at a position, showing a cel:
#
#   slot 0   the animation handle
#   slot 1   x, Q16.16
#   slot 2   y, Q16.16
#   slot 3   flags: visible, process animation, and marked for clearing
#   slot 4   which cel of the animation is showing
#
# The engine steps the cel of every processed entity once that animation's tick
# count has elapsed. A
# looping run wraps round; a run that does not loop stops on its last cel and the
# engine calls on-animation-end with the entity index, so "play this once" is
# just "set the animation and the cel to 0" - the end takes care of itself.
#
#   entity 0..99   board cells, row * 10 + column
#   entity 100     cursor box
#   entity 101     picked tile box
#
# A cell's resting position comes from its own index, so a tile that is falling
# only needs its current y: the entity drifts back towards row * cell. Because
# of that, a cell's y is not always row * cell - mid-animation it is wherever
# the tile it holds really is.
#
# What a cell holds is its kind, kept beside the pool. Matching compares kinds,
# not animations: an animation is what a gem looks like, and a gem that is
# glimmering looks briefly different without being a different gem.

# 24 * 65536, one cell in fixed point.
def cell
  1572864
end

# 40 * 65536: where the board starts horizontally.
def origin-x
  2621440
end

# Acceleration, Q16.16 pixels per tick per tick. 1500 px/s^2 over 1/60 s ticks is
# 0.4167 px/tick^2, so a tile falls the height of the board in about half a
# second, which is the pace the original game was tuned to.
def gravity
  27307
end

# One cell per ten ticks, so a swap takes a sixth of a second.
def swap-step
  157286
end

# Land a freshly spawned tile one whole board above its row.
def above-board
  15728640
end

# Entity constants come from the engine layout at compile time.
def stride
  %ENTITY_STRIDE
end

def slot-x
  %ENTITY_X
end

def slot-y
  %ENTITY_Y
end

def slot-flags
  %ENTITY_FLAGS
end

def slot-frame
  %ENTITY_FRAME
end

def slot-layer
  %ENTITY_LAYER
end

def slot-phase
  %ENTITY_PHASE
end

# Flag bits. The engine reads the first two; the rest are the game's.
def flag-visible
  %ENTITY_VISIBLE
end

def flag-process
  %ENTITY_PROCESS
end

def flag-marked
  %FLAG_GAME
end

# What a gem on the board carries, and what a marked one carries.
def gem-flags
  flag-visible flag-process +
end

def marked-flags
  gem-flags flag-marked +
end

# Draw order: the gems first, then the boxes over them.
def layer-board
  0
end

def layer-ui
  1
end

# Slot base of the two box entities (100 and 101), which the behaviour owns.
def cursor-base
  100 stride *
end

def picked-base
  101 stride *
end

# Ticks each cel is held for. The scene gives every animated sprite its own rate;
# these two must match it, and the behaviour compiler does not read the scene's.
def idle-ticks
  4
end

def glimmer-ticks
  4
end

# Cels in one idle run.
def idle-frames
  60
end

# Spacing between cells' starting cels. Anything coprime with the run length
# walks every cel before repeating, and a stride like this scatters them:
# neighbouring cells start far apart in the run instead of setting up a ripple
# across the board.
def phase-stride
  37
end

# Ticks one glimmer lasts: the whole glimmer run, played once.
def glimmer-length
  glimmer-ticks idle-frames *
end

# Ticks between glimmer rolls, at least this many plus the spread. Shorter than a
# glimmer, so runs overlap and more than one gem - and more than one of a kind -
# can be glimmering at once.
def glimmer-gap
  60
end

def glimmer-gap-spread
  120
end

# 6 + kind: the idle sprites are handles 6..11 and glimmer0..5 are 0..5, so a gem
# of kind k is idle at handle 6 + k and glimmers at handle k.
def idle-base
  $idle0
end

# Slot indices of the arrays the behaviour keeps, for indexing at run time.
def kind-base
  &kind0
end

def vel-base
  &velocity0
end

# Slot base of the board cell at (row, column) on the stack.
def at
  swap 10 * + stride *
end

# |x|
def abs
  dup 0 < [ neg ] when
end

# The kind entity i holds.
def kind-of
  kind-base + @
end

# Show animation h from cel n. i h cel -> i
#
# The animation slot is the first field, so it needs no offset. Both values have
# to go through temps: i is three deep by the time the stores are made. The
# engine's tick countdown is cleared with it, so the first cel gets its whole
# turn rather than whatever was left of the previous animation's.
def play
  !temp-a
  over stride * !
  @temp-a over stride * slot-frame + !
  0 over stride * slot-phase + !
end

# Give entity i its idle animation, from the first cel.
def play-idle
  dup kind-of idle-base + 0 play
end

# Give entity i its idle animation, from the cel that its cell starts on. Used
# when a gem appears, so the board does not breathe in step.
def play-idle-here
  dup phase-stride * idle-frames mod
  over kind-of idle-base + swap play
end

# Give entity i its glimmer animation, from the first cel.
def play-glimmer
  dup kind-of 0 play
end

# Move the cursor entity to the cell the cursor globals point at.
def place-cursor
  @cursor-column cell * origin-x +          # x
  @cursor-row cell *                      # x y
  cursor-base slot-y + !                  # store y
  cursor-base slot-x + !                  # store x
end

def move-up
  @cursor-row 1 - 0 max 9 min !cursor-row
end

def move-down
  @cursor-row 1 + 0 max 9 min !cursor-row
end

def move-left
  @cursor-column 1 - 0 max 9 min !cursor-column
end

def move-right
  @cursor-column 1 + 0 max 9 min !cursor-column
end

# Show the picked box on the cursor cell and remember where that was.
def pick
  @cursor-row !picked-row
  @cursor-column !picked-column
  @cursor-column cell * origin-x +          # x
  @cursor-row cell *                      # x y
  picked-base slot-y + !                  # store y
  picked-base slot-x + !                  # store x
  gem-flags picked-base slot-flags + !    # visible
end

def unpick
  -1 !picked-row
  -1 !picked-column
  0 picked-base slot-flags + !            # hidden
end

# Exchange what the picked and cursor cells hold: the animation, the cel it is
# showing, and the kind. Not the positions - the two entities slide, and where
# they end up is what the swap is.
#
# A gem therefore keeps its own animation and its own place in the run when it
# changes cells, which is the point of holding both.
def swap-tiles
  @cursor-row @cursor-column at !temp-a           # slot base of the cursor cell
  @picked-row @picked-column at !temp-b           # slot base of the picked cell
  @temp-a @ !temp-c
  @temp-b @ !temp-d
  @temp-c @temp-b !
  @temp-d @temp-a !
  @temp-a slot-frame + @ !temp-c
  @temp-b slot-frame + @ !temp-d
  @temp-c @temp-b slot-frame + !
  @temp-d @temp-a slot-frame + !
  @temp-a stride / kind-base + @ !temp-c
  @temp-b stride / kind-base + @ !temp-d
  @temp-c @temp-b stride / kind-base + !
  @temp-d @temp-a stride / kind-base + !
end

# Only neighbours may be swapped.
def adjacent
  @picked-row @cursor-row - abs
  @picked-column @cursor-column - abs
  + 1 =
end

# Slide the two cells towards each other; what they hold changes hands at the
# end.
#
# The two slot bases go in named slots rather than the scratch ones because they
# have to survive until the slide finishes, and a glimmer may be rolled or an
# animation may end in between.
def swap-start
  @cursor-row @cursor-column at !swap-a
  @picked-row @picked-column at !swap-b
  @picked-column @cursor-column - swap-step * !step-x
  @picked-row @cursor-row - swap-step * !step-y
  0 picked-base slot-flags + !            # hide the picked box while it slides
  10 !timer
  1 !phase
end

def swap-tick
  @swap-a slot-x + @ @step-x + @swap-a slot-x + !
  @swap-a slot-y + @ @step-y + @swap-a slot-y + !
  0 @step-x - @swap-b slot-x + @ + @swap-b slot-x + !
  0 @step-y - @swap-b slot-y + @ + @swap-b slot-y + !
  @timer 1 - !timer
  @timer 0 <= [ swap-finish ] when
end

def swap-finish
  @cursor-column cell * origin-x + @swap-a slot-x + !
  @cursor-row cell * @swap-a slot-y + !
  @picked-column cell * origin-x + @swap-b slot-x + !
  @picked-row cell * @swap-b slot-y + !
  swap-tiles
  unpick
  1 !dirty
  0 !phase
end

# Does cell j hold a gem of the same kind as the one in temp-a, and hold one at
# all?
def same-kind
  dup stride * @ 0 >=
  swap kind-of @temp-a =
  and
end

# Mark the three cells starting at i, stepping by the number of cells in temp-b.
def mark-run
  marked-flags over stride * slot-flags + !
  marked-flags over @temp-b + stride * slot-flags + !
  marked-flags over @temp-b 2 * + stride * slot-flags + !
  1 !matches
end

# If the three cells starting at i, stepping by step cells, hold the same kind,
# mark all three. Leaves i on the stack so the caller can go on using it.
def mark3
  !temp-b                                   # temp-b is the step, in cells
  dup !temp-c                               # temp-c is i
  dup kind-of !temp-a                       # temp-a is the kind
  @temp-c same-kind
  @temp-c @temp-b + same-kind
  @temp-c @temp-b 2 * + same-kind
  and and
  [ mark-run ] when
end

# Mark every cell that belongs to a run.
def match-pass
  0 !matches
  for 100 [
    dup 10 mod 8 < [ 1 mark3 ] when
    dup 10 / 8 < [ 10 mark3 ] when
    drop
  ]
end

# Hide every marked cell.
def clear-pass
  for 100 [
    dup stride * slot-flags + @ flag-marked / 2 mod
    [
      -1 over stride * !
      gem-flags over stride * slot-flags + !
    ] when
    drop
  ]
end

# Move source i into the current destination, keeping its position so it falls.
def compact-move
  dup stride * !temp-a
  @collapse-destination stride * !temp-b
  @temp-a @ @temp-b !
  @temp-a slot-frame + @ @temp-b slot-frame + !
  @temp-a slot-phase + @ @temp-b slot-phase + !
  @temp-a slot-y + @ @temp-b slot-y + !
  dup kind-of @collapse-destination kind-base + !
  vel-base over + @ vel-base @collapse-destination + !
  0 vel-base over + !
  -1 @temp-a !
end

# Visit each column from bottom to top. Each occupied cell consumes one
# destination, so surviving gems keep their order and refill still runs in
# board order.
def collapse-pass
  for 10 [
    dup 90 + !collapse-destination
    for 10 [
      90 swap 10 * - @collapse-destination 10 mod +
      dup stride * @ 0 >= [
        dup @collapse-destination = not [ compact-move ] when
        @collapse-destination 10 - !collapse-destination
      ] when
      drop
    ]
    drop
  ]
end

# New tiles enter from above the board, keeping their spacing, so a refilled
# column reads as one stack falling in.
def refill-pass
  for 100 [
    dup stride * @ 0 <
    [
      random 6 mod over kind-base + !                 # a new kind
      play-idle-here
      dup 10 / cell * above-board - over stride * slot-y + !
      vel-base over + !temp-d
      0 @temp-d !
      gem-flags over stride * slot-flags + !
    ] when
    drop
  ]
end

# One tick of gravity for one cell. Leaves i on the stack.
def fall-entity
  dup 10 / cell * !temp-b                 # temp-b is where it comes to rest
  vel-base over + !temp-d                 # temp-d is velocity[i]
  dup stride * slot-y + @ !temp-c         # temp-c is y
  @temp-c @temp-b < [
    @temp-d @ gravity + @temp-d !
    @temp-c @temp-d @ + !temp-c
    @temp-c @temp-b >= [
      @temp-b !temp-c
      0 @temp-d !
    ] when
    1 !moving
  ] when
  @temp-c over stride * slot-y + !
end

def fall-tick
  0 !moving
  for 100 [ fall-entity drop ]
  @moving 0 = [ 0 !phase 1 !dirty ] when
end

def clear-tick
  @timer 1 - !timer
  @timer 0 <= [
    clear-pass
    collapse-pass
    refill-pass
    3 !phase
  ] when
end

def idle-tick
  @dirty 0 >
  [
    match-pass
    0 !dirty
    @matches 0 > [ 8 !timer 2 !phase ] when
  ] when
end

# Roll for a glimmer. Any cell may be picked, including one that is already
# glimmering - it is simply left alone, so several can run at once and two gems
# of a kind can be glimmering together.
def roll-glimmer
  random 100 mod
  dup stride * @ idle-base >=
    [ play-glimmer drop ]
    [ drop ]
  ifelse
  @time glimmer-gap + random glimmer-gap-spread mod + !next-roll
end

def tick
  @time 1 + !time
  @time @next-roll >= [ roll-glimmer ] when
  @phase 0 = [ idle-tick ] when
  @phase 1 = [ swap-tick ] when
  @phase 2 = [ clear-tick ] when
  @phase 3 = [ fall-tick ] when
end

# The engine calls this when an entity's run reaches its last cel, with the
# entity as the argument. A glimmer is played once, so this is where the gem
# goes back to its idle animation, from the beginning.
#
# No scratch slots: this runs between ticks, when a swap may be part way through.
def on-animation-end
  dup stride * @                          # entity, animation
  dup 0 >=
  over idle-base <
  and
    [ drop play-idle drop ]
    [ drop drop ]
  ifelse
end

# Build the world: lay out the board, then put the boxes in place. Called once.
#
# The index of each cell is pushed by 'for'; the body leaves nothing behind.
def init
  for 100 [
    random 6 mod over kind-base + !                 # kind
    play-idle-here
    dup 10 mod cell * origin-x + over stride * slot-x + !
    dup 10 / cell * over stride * slot-y + !
    layer-board over stride * slot-layer + !
    gem-flags over stride * slot-flags + !
    drop
  ]
  $cursor cursor-base !                      # cursor animation
  $picked picked-base !                      # picked animation
  layer-ui cursor-base slot-layer + !
  layer-ui picked-base slot-layer + !
  gem-flags cursor-base slot-flags + !       # cursor visible
  place-cursor
end

# Keys arrive as BIOS scan codes for the arrows and 13 for return. `dup 72 =`
# consumes the copy, so the key stays on the stack for the remaining tests and
# the quotations must leave it alone; the final drop removes it.
#
# Input is ignored while the board is animating, so a key cannot land in the
# middle of a swap or a cascade.
def on-key
  @phase 0 = [
    dup 72 = [ move-up ] when
    dup 80 = [ move-down ] when
    dup 75 = [ move-left ] when
    dup 77 = [ move-right ] when
    dup 13 = [ toggle-pick ] when
    drop
    place-cursor
  ] [ drop ] ifelse
end

# Return picks a tile; return on a neighbour starts the swap, and on anything
# else moves the pick instead of clearing it.
def toggle-pick
  @picked-row 0 <
    [ pick ]
    [ adjacent [ swap-start ] [ pick ] ifelse ]
  ifelse
end
